WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.435
-Our first guest tonight
is an Academy Award-

00:00:02.435 --> 00:00:04.237
and Tony-nominated actor,
writer, and director

00:00:04.237 --> 00:00:06.105
you know from films
such as "Training Day,"

00:00:06.105 --> 00:00:07.807
"Boyhood," and
"The Magnificent Seven."

00:00:07.807 --> 00:00:11.177
His graphic novel, "Meadowlark,"
is available now.

00:00:11.177 --> 00:00:13.479
Please welcome back to the show
our good friend Ethan Hawke.

00:00:13.479 --> 00:00:15.047
Hello, Ethan!
How are you?

00:00:15.047 --> 00:00:16.649
-Hey, Seth!
How are you doing?

00:00:16.649 --> 00:00:19.185
Welcome to Budapest.
-I mean, I miss you.

00:00:19.185 --> 00:00:20.653
I don't like when
you're this far away

00:00:20.653 --> 00:00:22.222
on the other side
of the Atlantic Ocean.

00:00:22.222 --> 00:00:24.290
Are you enjoying
your time there so far?

00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:26.759
-I am, but I love New York,
and I love my family,

00:00:26.759 --> 00:00:28.361
and I wish I was there.

00:00:28.361 --> 00:00:31.197
-You're there shooting
"Moon Knight"

00:00:31.197 --> 00:00:32.932
with Oscar Isaac for Disney+.

00:00:32.932 --> 00:00:36.669
And I will say --
Look, you're indie-film royalty.

00:00:36.669 --> 00:00:38.871
You know that.
You might not want to admit it.

00:00:38.871 --> 00:00:41.807
You've done some big films, too,
but I got to imagine

00:00:41.807 --> 00:00:43.576
this is sort of unlike anything
you've done before,

00:00:43.576 --> 00:00:46.079
being in the Marvel Universe.

00:00:46.079 --> 00:00:49.381
-Well, one of the first things
that made it unlike anything

00:00:49.381 --> 00:00:53.919
I've ever done before is,
I had to sign like 10,000 NDAs

00:00:53.919 --> 00:00:56.556
about what I would and would not
say, which I've never done,

00:00:56.556 --> 00:00:58.625
and I don't respect,
and I don't honor.

00:00:58.625 --> 00:01:00.293
So I'm happy to talk to you
about it completely.

00:01:00.293 --> 00:01:02.161
-[ Laughs ]
-No, yeah.

00:01:02.161 --> 00:01:06.332
I'll tell you this about it.
I've been acting since I was 13.

00:01:06.332 --> 00:01:09.202
Like, I did my first movie
in 1985, okay?

00:01:09.202 --> 00:01:10.837
And that's a long time ago.

00:01:10.837 --> 00:01:15.141
And these are the best sets
I've ever been on in my life.

00:01:15.141 --> 00:01:17.777
The costumes, the set,
the production design,

00:01:17.777 --> 00:01:20.379
the cinematography,
all those elements

00:01:20.379 --> 00:01:23.182
are kind of mind-boggling.

00:01:23.182 --> 00:01:26.552
So I have entered
some alternate universe,

00:01:26.552 --> 00:01:28.754
and it is kind of interesting.

00:01:28.754 --> 00:01:32.659
-I will say this, and I mean, if
there's an indie-film character

00:01:32.659 --> 00:01:34.694
in the Marvel Universe,
I feel like Moon Knight

00:01:34.694 --> 00:01:35.929
might be that character,

00:01:35.929 --> 00:01:38.731
so I do kind of feel like
this is a nice fit.

00:01:38.731 --> 00:01:43.069
-Well, I heard about it
from Oscar Isaac, who lives

00:01:43.069 --> 00:01:45.304
like, you know,
three blocks down the street

00:01:45.304 --> 00:01:47.473
from me in Brooklyn.
I was at a coffee shop,

00:01:47.473 --> 00:01:48.641
and he came up to me,
he's like,

00:01:48.641 --> 00:01:50.109
"Hey, I really liked
'The Good Lord Bird.'"

00:01:50.109 --> 00:01:51.911
I was like, "Oh, cool, yeah.
I really like your work.

00:01:51.911 --> 00:01:54.380
You're amazing."
He's like, "Hey, want to be

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:56.983
in the 'Moon Knight' with me?"
I was like, "Yeah."

00:01:56.983 --> 00:01:58.184
[ Laughter ]

00:01:58.184 --> 00:02:00.386
So it happened the right way,
you know?

00:02:00.386 --> 00:02:03.856
-Yeah. Born in Brooklyn,
shoots in Budapest.

00:02:03.856 --> 00:02:07.093
-Yeah. And, you know,
we're conjuring ancient gods,

00:02:07.093 --> 00:02:08.594
and we're fighting
for good and evil.

00:02:08.594 --> 00:02:11.130
And it's very exciting.
-I thought --

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:13.065
By the way, I should note
that with those glasses,

00:02:13.065 --> 00:02:15.401
your long hair,
and those candles,

00:02:15.401 --> 00:02:18.204
you're giving off
a real David Koresh vibe.

00:02:18.204 --> 00:02:20.673
[ Laughter ]

00:02:20.673 --> 00:02:22.809
-I've based my character
on David Koresh.

00:02:22.809 --> 00:02:24.643
-Have you really?
-Yeah.

00:02:24.643 --> 00:02:27.513
So, I mean, I --
I guess it's working.

00:02:27.513 --> 00:02:30.049
I totally --
I wanted a Camaro, you know?

00:02:30.049 --> 00:02:32.752
I want to ride in
in a Camaro.

00:02:32.752 --> 00:02:35.921
Yeah, I can't believe you --
I mean, you're good, Seth.

00:02:35.921 --> 00:02:37.056
You're good.
-Yeah.

00:02:37.056 --> 00:02:39.024
-Or maybe I'm not
out of character yet.

00:02:39.024 --> 00:02:42.228
-I should say
I was a huge fan of Mr. Koresh!

00:02:42.228 --> 00:02:44.229
[ Laughter ]

00:02:44.229 --> 00:02:47.433
-I don't know if we want to be
on record saying that.

00:02:47.433 --> 00:02:49.369
I will say that he is the basis

00:02:49.369 --> 00:02:51.804
of great character inspiration,
however.

00:02:51.804 --> 00:02:55.241
-Yes, I think that is very fair.
Hey, you talk about the fact --

00:02:55.241 --> 00:02:57.510
I want to ask, 'cause we've been
talking about how some people

00:02:57.510 --> 00:03:00.146
now are starting
to fake vaccine cards, right?

00:03:00.146 --> 00:03:02.048
That's a very real thing.
You mentioned

00:03:02.048 --> 00:03:04.884
your first film when you were
15, and I know you weren't --

00:03:04.884 --> 00:03:07.153
You didn't become world-famous
off your first film,

00:03:07.153 --> 00:03:11.857
but did Ethan Hawke
ever have to have a fake I.D.?

00:03:11.857 --> 00:03:16.229
-Oh, definitely.
Well, see, the good news for me

00:03:16.229 --> 00:03:19.832
was that my first movie that
I did was a complete failure,

00:03:19.832 --> 00:03:22.401
and nobody ever heard of it
or knew who I was.

00:03:22.401 --> 00:03:23.869
So, I mean,
I don't know where you --

00:03:23.869 --> 00:03:26.706
The fake I.D./fake vaccine card
is very interesting.

00:03:26.706 --> 00:03:29.541
But not only did
I have a fake I.D.,

00:03:29.541 --> 00:03:32.177
I had a backup,
because I looked really young.

00:03:32.177 --> 00:03:35.047
You know, I didn't have much
facial hair, anything like that.

00:03:35.047 --> 00:03:37.483
And I went to 42nd Street,
and I got --

00:03:37.483 --> 00:03:40.286
It said, "Ethan Hawke."
They said, "Where do you live?"

00:03:40.286 --> 00:03:42.555
And I said -- I had to make up
a New York address.

00:03:42.555 --> 00:03:45.624
The only thing I could think of
was "Monopoly," you know?

00:03:45.624 --> 00:03:48.327
I said, you know, Park Avenue.

00:03:48.327 --> 00:03:52.165
And I know "Monopoly"
is not set in New York,

00:03:52.165 --> 00:03:54.133
but it is what I thought of.

00:03:54.133 --> 00:03:58.871
So I was Ethan Hawke
who lived at 500 Park Place

00:03:58.871 --> 00:04:03.710
and -- and people bought it.
I mean, when people want

00:04:03.710 --> 00:04:05.445
to make money off, you know,
kids buying beer,

00:04:05.445 --> 00:04:07.513
they'll generally --
if you've got the fake I.D.,

00:04:07.513 --> 00:04:09.148
it's your fault,
not theirs, right?

00:04:09.148 --> 00:04:10.383
-Right, if you put the work in,

00:04:10.383 --> 00:04:12.618
they're willing
to just look the other way.

00:04:12.618 --> 00:04:14.653
-Also, I had the backup,
which was a fake

00:04:14.653 --> 00:04:17.857
Rutgers University card,
which I happened to really like

00:04:17.857 --> 00:04:19.124
the photo of myself in.

00:04:19.124 --> 00:04:20.860
I thought I looked
really cool and tough.

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:22.262
-[ Laughs ]

00:04:22.262 --> 00:04:25.598
-And it was largely there
to impress girls.

00:04:25.598 --> 00:04:29.034
-But, like, how quickly after
"Dead Poets" did you --

00:04:29.034 --> 00:04:33.206
could you walk into
pretty much any bar?

00:04:33.206 --> 00:04:38.211
-Well, the trouble was that
I was living in New York City

00:04:38.211 --> 00:04:39.778
after "Dead Poets," right?
-Yeah.

00:04:39.778 --> 00:04:41.981
-I moved there.
And New York City,

00:04:41.981 --> 00:04:44.683
one of the sad realities
of it is,

00:04:44.683 --> 00:04:48.388
those delis
don't really card very much.

00:04:48.388 --> 00:04:49.655
-I see.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:49.655 --> 00:04:52.157
-And I was a pretty
low-rent date, Seth.

00:04:52.157 --> 00:04:53.492
I wasn't going to bars,
you know?

00:04:53.492 --> 00:04:55.461
I wasn't going
to Studio 54 and stuff.

00:04:55.461 --> 00:04:58.765
I was, you know,
buying a six-pack

00:04:58.765 --> 00:05:00.466
and taking it back
and playing the guitar.

00:05:00.466 --> 00:05:03.369
That was more my thing.
-Well, you have your --

00:05:03.369 --> 00:05:06.305
You have your Texas roots,
and that is --

00:05:06.305 --> 00:05:08.574
You kind of pulled
on your Texas roots

00:05:08.574 --> 00:05:12.745
to write this fantastic
graphic novel, and Greg Ruth --

00:05:12.745 --> 00:05:14.480
Is it pronounced
"Ruh-th" or "Rooth"?

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:15.614
-Greg "Rooth."
-"Rooth."

00:05:15.614 --> 00:05:19.051
-He's the genius
behind the whole operation.

00:05:19.051 --> 00:05:22.088
I'm kind of the star
of a graphic novel.

00:05:22.088 --> 00:05:24.223
I helped write it.
He's the director,

00:05:24.223 --> 00:05:27.293
he's the costume designer,
he's the hair-and-makeup artist.

00:05:27.293 --> 00:05:30.062
He's, you know, I mean --
It's his artistry

00:05:30.062 --> 00:05:31.797
that really makes it,
and I'm so grateful

00:05:31.797 --> 00:05:33.732
to be on your show
even talking about it, Seth.

00:05:33.732 --> 00:05:35.968
I really appreciate it.
I'm so proud of this book.

00:05:35.968 --> 00:05:38.003
-Well, we are big fans
of graphic novels here,

00:05:38.003 --> 00:05:39.805
and you're not lying
when you say you're the star,

00:05:39.805 --> 00:05:42.642
'cause you're kind of --
I mean, he definitely drew it

00:05:42.642 --> 00:05:44.209
to be Ethan Hawke.

00:05:44.209 --> 00:05:46.211
Did he know your face enough,
or did you have to do

00:05:46.211 --> 00:05:49.014
some modelling work
for the book?

00:05:49.014 --> 00:05:51.417
-We're pretty good friends.
We did another graphic novel

00:05:51.417 --> 00:05:54.019
called "Indeh" first,
and after "Indeh" was over,

00:05:54.019 --> 00:05:56.555
we wanted to do something --
That was historical fiction.

00:05:56.555 --> 00:05:59.258
It was about the Apache wars,
the life of Geronimo,

00:05:59.258 --> 00:06:01.627
and that whole situation
that happened down

00:06:01.627 --> 00:06:05.998
in the Northwest --
I mean, the Southwest, sorry.

00:06:05.998 --> 00:06:10.369
And so, this was -- it was born
right after I had finished

00:06:10.369 --> 00:06:13.506
shooting "Magnificent Seven,"
and I had this big, long goatee

00:06:13.506 --> 00:06:16.108
in "Mag Seven,"
and when I chopped it off,

00:06:16.108 --> 00:06:20.780
I had a little kind of
Fu Manchu mustache, so to speak.

00:06:20.780 --> 00:06:23.548
And Greg --
I sent Greg some pictures.

00:06:23.548 --> 00:06:25.084
He's like,
"That's gonna be your --

00:06:25.084 --> 00:06:29.055
That's your look for the graphic
novel!" and he just worked it.

00:06:29.055 --> 00:06:32.758
-It's a really great,
very exciting book.

00:06:32.758 --> 00:06:34.059
It takes place over one day,

00:06:34.059 --> 00:06:37.497
but it's really, at its core,
a father/son story.

00:06:37.497 --> 00:06:40.132
Is this based
on your relationships,

00:06:40.132 --> 00:06:43.402
Greg's relationship,
both of your relationships?

00:06:43.402 --> 00:06:46.572
-Well, we each had sons
that were turning 16

00:06:46.572 --> 00:06:49.875
when we first started.
And we --

00:06:49.875 --> 00:06:51.344
Because we're friends,

00:06:51.344 --> 00:06:53.312
we talked about this
strange thing that happens.

00:06:53.312 --> 00:06:55.114
You have a young person,

00:06:55.114 --> 00:06:57.583
a young boy,
and he's growing up,

00:06:57.583 --> 00:06:59.919
and he looks up to you so much,
and he thinks you're --

00:06:59.919 --> 00:07:03.189
You're dad, you know?
And then, this weird thing

00:07:03.189 --> 00:07:06.024
called adolescence happens,
and all of a sudden,

00:07:06.024 --> 00:07:08.994
they stand up,
and they're as tall as you are,

00:07:08.994 --> 00:07:11.997
and they're looking at you
in the eyes,

00:07:11.997 --> 00:07:13.766
and there's
this profound feeling

00:07:13.766 --> 00:07:16.435
of disappointment
coming from them, you know?

00:07:16.435 --> 00:07:17.770
That they really wish --

00:07:17.770 --> 00:07:20.072
"You mean this is
who I got stuck with?"

00:07:20.072 --> 00:07:22.007
[ Laughter ]
"You're my dad?

00:07:22.007 --> 00:07:24.677
Like, couldn't you --"
And they're there to call out

00:07:24.677 --> 00:07:26.946
everything fake
you've ever thought,

00:07:26.946 --> 00:07:29.982
any way that your behavior
is hypocritical,

00:07:29.982 --> 00:07:34.854
and you have this sense of just
being such a disappointment.

00:07:34.854 --> 00:07:37.690
You know, you can't lead them
any farther than you've gone

00:07:37.690 --> 00:07:41.327
in your own development,
and to Greg and I,

00:07:41.327 --> 00:07:44.897
as we started talking about it,
it felt kind of Greek to us,

00:07:44.897 --> 00:07:47.733
and we had this idea of,
"What if we wrote a book

00:07:47.733 --> 00:07:52.471
that was a father/son story,
but at its secret subconscious

00:07:52.471 --> 00:07:54.606
was kind of a Greek myth?"
And we set it --

00:07:54.606 --> 00:07:58.143
We're both from Texas,
so we're kind of hodgepodging

00:07:58.143 --> 00:08:01.613
our own childhood
with our sons' childhood

00:08:01.613 --> 00:08:03.849
and our experiences as a father

00:08:03.849 --> 00:08:06.252
and trying to write
great crime fiction, you know?

00:08:06.252 --> 00:08:09.521
Like, to set a myth
as if it was crime fiction.

00:08:09.521 --> 00:08:12.624
Which is what I think, you know,
a lot of the best writing --

00:08:12.624 --> 00:08:15.961
Jim Thompson's one of my
favorite crime writers, and so,

00:08:15.961 --> 00:08:17.696
I had just read a Jim Thompson
book, and I was like,

00:08:17.696 --> 00:08:20.266
"What if we could channel
our inner Jim Thompson

00:08:20.266 --> 00:08:22.367
and make a really original
graphic novel?"

00:08:22.367 --> 00:08:24.871
So that was
the thesis behind it.

